WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.090
The white picket fence.

00:00:02.090 --> 00:00:03.550
Space for entertaining.

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:05.860
Acres of land to call your own.

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:08.810
These were characteristics of the American
dream home.

00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:21.650
But is it still a dream worth chasing?

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:23.420
Should you buy a home or rent?

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:27.360
We're here at the White House at 6 Woods Lane
in Easthampton Long Island.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:31.420
You could own this place for the list price
of $12.5 million.

00:00:31.429 --> 00:00:37.690
With a 30-year fixed rate mortgage 3.991%
interest, you'd pay nearly $47000 a month,

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:41.989
and that's not including closing costs, home
ownership insurance, or property taxes.

00:00:41.989 --> 00:00:46.070
Now, this kind of place might be a little
pricey for you, but the same principles apply

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:49.920
at much lower levels when you're considering
whether to rent or buy a home.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:56.239
Now, before I break something in here, let's
get back to the studio.

00:00:56.239 --> 00:00:58.659
To buy or not to buy?

00:00:58.659 --> 00:01:01.079
That is the question.

00:01:01.079 --> 00:01:03.739
We polled our YouTube audience, and they had
a lot to say.

00:01:03.739 --> 00:01:07.090
One person said owning a house should not
be thought of as an investment.

00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:09.680
More like something between an asset and a
liability.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:13.910
Someone else shared a pretty common sentiment
these days that student loans would prevent

00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:15.600
them from ever buying a home.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.320
Another said abolish private property altogether.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:19.670
But, okay.

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:24.610
A majority of you think ownership is the way
to go, and who can blame them?

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:27.880
People think of buying a home as part of the
American dream.

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:29.840
The driver of middle class wealth.

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:31.500
A sound investment vehicle.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:34.310
Renting a place is less romantic.

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:37.820
Throwing money away every month on a place
to rest your head and keep your stuff, but

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:39.240
that calculus is changing.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:43.630
To help us run down the reasons why you may
want to rent and not buy, I spoke to financial

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:44.860
planner Roger Ma.

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:47.050
Let's talk about renting versus buying.

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:48.050
Sure.

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:51.310
So, can you tell me why renting is not throwing
your money away?

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:55.920
Well, I think the flip side is why is buying
not wasting money?

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:00.700
And I think that's a myth that when you buy,
a lot of your money is building equity, but

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:01.940
that's just not the case.

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:07.360
Your monthly payment, and the upfront, and
the backend cost of buying are wasted money

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:08.360
as well.

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:14.820
You've got mortgage interest, homeowners insurance,
property taxes, potentially homeowners association

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:15.820
fees.

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:21.770
Not to mention the huge closing cost on the
front end and the back end.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:28.500
If you don't plan to stay in the same area
for at least five years, it probably makes

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:33.060
more sense to rent, and that's because of
those large closing costs.

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:38.630
You need to give your home a sufficient amount
of time to potentially increase in value.

00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:44.160
I feel like the generation now growing is
maybe more likely to be on the move.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:45.160
Definitely.

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:49.360
Between the ages of 22 and 40, the average
person changes jobs every one to two years,

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:51.500
so they're changing a fair amount.

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:57.960
So, I think having the flexibility to change
jobs could be reasons to rent instead of buy.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:02:59.960
And that's okay.

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:02.930
There's no shame in renting, and if you look
around the United States, depending on where

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:05.900
you live, it might actually be more affordable.

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:11.290
ATTOM Data Solutions publishes a yearly affordability
index of buying verse renting, and in most

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:16.230
places, 59% to be exact, real estate prices
are outpacing rentals.

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:21.680
In fact, in all 18 of the most populous counties
in America, it's more affordable to rent than

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:22.680
buy.

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:23.680
Lets be real.

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:26.650
For a lot of people, the choice of renting
verse buying may not even be yours, especially

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:27.650
if you're young.

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:31.840
I talked to Bloomberg reporter Patrick Clark
about the unique problems facing potential

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:32.910
home buyers.

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:38.230
Let's talk about the unique hindrances that
maybe new home buyers might face.

00:03:38.230 --> 00:03:40.100
There's not enough homes they can afford.

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:41.100
Right?

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:42.100
Sure.

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:43.100
That would be a big problem.

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:44.980
At the very most basic, there's an affordability
gap.

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:48.680
A decade after the foreclosure crisis, we're
still building homes.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:53.270
Not just of where they were at the peak bubble
madness, but still below the number of new

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:55.820
homes that we were adding in the mid '90s.

00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:59.900
The construction that is happening has skewed towards the high end.

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:05.640
That is sort of pressured on the other side
by maybe age, people growing older and maybe

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:07.520
not moving out of their homes.

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:09.430
There's two different ways you can get listings,
right?

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:15.390
You could build new homes or you can have
homes that were already occupied hit the market.

00:04:15.390 --> 00:04:20.960
There's some question of whether people want
to stay in their own homes and "age in place".

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:28.170
Older people staying in their homes longer
is an issue, so there's some gridlock in the

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:29.170
housing market.

00:04:29.170 --> 00:04:36.599
This generation is now saddled with more burdens
than previously was the case.

00:04:36.599 --> 00:04:37.599
Like way more.

00:04:37.599 --> 00:04:42.710
There's more than $1.5 trillion of student
loan debt outstanding.

00:04:42.710 --> 00:04:43.710
Yikes.

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:46.389
The deck is certainly stacked against this
generation.

00:04:46.389 --> 00:04:51.129
But that long standing elusive goal of home
ownership seems so tempting.

00:04:51.129 --> 00:04:56.090
All in all, I'd have to put it this way: If
you have your heart set on home ownership,

00:04:56.090 --> 00:04:57.800
nobody's going to stand in your way.

00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:04.120
But you probably don't need to sweat it too
much either if you don't.

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:05.349
That was probably expensive.

